## p3-wav2 | Waves and sound | physics | 1 | phys3 | -
explain 2 | Every wave obeys v = fλ
- Speed = frequency × wavelength.
- Sound in air: about 343 m/s. Middle C (262 Hz) has a wavelength of about 1.3 m.
- Light: 3 × 10⁸ m/s. Green light (550 nm) oscillates about 5.5 × 10¹⁴ times a second.
> Sound needs a medium, light doesn't

quiz 2 | Count seconds between lightning and thunder: 6 s. Roughly how far away?
* About 2 km
o About 6 km
o About 200 m
o About 20 km
= Sound covers ~343 m per second: 6 × 343 ≈ 2 km.
= Light arrives essentially instantly.
> Loudness is logarithmic

explain 3 | Decibels are logarithmic
- +10 dB means 10× the intensity. +20 dB means 100×.
- A rock concert (110 dB) is a billion times the intensity of a whisper (20 dB).
- *Your ears perceive roughly every +10 dB as "twice as loud".*
> Beats: two close notes make a wobble

quiz 3 | Tuning forks of 440 Hz and 444 Hz sound together. What do you hear?
* A 442 Hz tone pulsing 4 times a second
o Two separate notes
o An 884 Hz tone
o A 4 Hz hum
= Superposition gives an average frequency with an amplitude envelope at the difference frequency.
= Musicians tune by listening for beats to slow and vanish.
> Breaking the sound barrier makes a cone of sound

explain 3 | Sonic booms
- A plane faster than sound outruns its own pressure waves.
- They pile up into a cone-shaped shock front: the Mach cone, with sin θ = v_sound/v.
- *The boom follows the plane along the ground the whole time it's supersonic,* not just once.
> Next: interference and diffraction

## p3-int | Interference and diffraction | physics | 2 | phys3 | p3-wave
quiz 3 | Double slit: slit spacing d, wavelength λ, screen distance L. Fringe spacing?
* λL/d
o dL/λ
o λd/L
o λ/d
= Bright fringes where path difference d sin θ = mλ. Small angles give spacing λL/d.
= Closer slits spread the pattern out.
> Young's experiment was evidence for light as a wave in 1801

story 4 | Thomas Young fought Newton's ghost
- Newton had argued light was particles. His reputation made the idea hard to challenge.
- Around 1801–1803 Young showed interference fringes with light, which only waves can make.
- *A century later, quantum theory said both of them were partly right.*
> Thin films make rainbow colours

real 3 | Why soap bubbles and oil slicks shimmer
- Light reflects off the top and bottom of a thin film and the two reflections interfere.
- Which wavelengths reinforce depends on film thickness, so colours shift as the film thins.
- *Just before a bubble pops, it goes black: too thin for any visible light to reinforce.*
> Diffraction limits how sharply anything can see

explain 4 | The diffraction limit
- A lens of diameter D can't resolve angles smaller than about 1.22λ/D (Rayleigh criterion).
- Bigger telescopes see finer detail. So do shorter wavelengths.
- *Electron microscopes beat light microscopes because electrons have far shorter wavelengths.*
> CDs and DVDs are diffraction gratings

quiz 3 | Why does a Blu-ray hold more than a DVD?
* It uses a shorter-wavelength (blue-violet) laser, so pits can be smaller
o It spins faster
o It's physically bigger
o It uses both sides
= 405 nm versus 650 nm. A smaller diffraction-limited spot means denser data.
> Next: heat and temperature

## p3-th1 | Laws of thermodynamics | physics | 1 | phys3 | p3-int
explain 2 | The four laws of thermodynamics
- Zeroth: things in equilibrium with a third thing are in equilibrium with each other (temperature makes sense).
- First: energy is conserved. ΔU = Q − W.
- Second: entropy of an isolated system never decreases.
- Third: you can't reach absolute zero in finitely many steps.
> A famous summary: you can't win, you can't break even, you can't quit

quiz 3 | 1 kg of water warmed by 10°C. Heat needed? (c = 4,200 J/kg·K)
* 42,000 J
o 4,200 J
o 420 J
o 420,000 J
= Q = mcΔT.
= Water's high heat capacity is why oceans moderate coastal climates.
> Phase changes eat energy without changing temperature

explain 3 | Latent heat
- Melting ice at 0°C absorbs about 334 kJ/kg without warming at all.
- Boiling water absorbs about 2,260 kJ/kg.
- *That's why steam burns are worse than boiling-water burns.*
> Absolute zero is −273.15°C

story 4 | Kelvin realised there's a bottom to temperature
- Gases shrink by a fixed fraction per degree. Extrapolate and their pressure hits zero at −273.15°C.
- William Thomson (Lord Kelvin) built a temperature scale from that absolute zero in 1848.
- *The coldest labs now reach billionths of a kelvin, but never zero.*
> Next: molecules in motion

## p3-kin | Kinetic theory | physics | 2 | phys3 | p3-th1
explain 3 | Temperature is average kinetic energy
- For a monatomic ideal gas: ⟨½mv²⟩ = (3/2)k_BT.
- Air molecules at room temperature move at around 500 m/s.
- *Pressure is the drumming of trillions of collisions on the walls.*
> pV = nRT falls out of collisions

quiz 3 | Double the absolute temperature of a gas at fixed volume. Pressure…
* Doubles
o Quadruples
o Stays the same
o Increases by √2
= pV = nRT, so p ∝ T.
= Molecular speed rises by √2, and both collision rate and impulse per collision scale with speed.
> Brownian motion proved atoms exist

story 5 | Einstein used pollen jitter to prove atoms exist (1905)
- Robert Brown saw pollen particles jiggling in water in 1827, without explanation.
- Einstein showed random molecular kicks predict a specific diffusion rate.
- Jean Perrin measured it in 1908, giving Avogadro's number. *The last atom sceptics gave up.*
> The Maxwell–Boltzmann distribution

explain 4 | Speeds follow the Maxwell–Boltzmann distribution
- Few molecules are slow, few are very fast; most are in between.
- The high-speed tail grows steeply with temperature.
- *Reaction rates depend on that tail, which is why cooking is so sensitive to temperature.*
> Next: engines and refrigerators

## p3-eng | Heat engines | physics | 2 | phys3 | p3-kin
explain 3 | No engine can turn all heat into work
- A heat engine takes heat from a hot source, does work, and dumps the rest to a cold sink.
- Carnot: max efficiency 1 − T_c/T_h.
- *Car engines manage roughly 25–40%. Big combined-cycle power stations reach around 60%.*
> Heat pumps beat 100%

quiz 4 | A heat pump moves heat from 0°C outside to a 20°C house. Ideal heat delivered per joule of work?
* About 15 J
o 1 J
o Less than 1 J
o About 2 J
= Ideal COP = T_h/(T_h − T_c) = 293/20 ≈ 15.
= Real ones manage 3–4: still 3–4 J of heat per joule of electricity.
> Entropy explains why

quiz 3 | Melting 1 kg of ice at 0°C (L = 334 kJ/kg). Entropy change of the ice?
* About +1,220 J/K
o About −1,220 J/K
o Zero
o About +334,000 J/K
= ΔS = Q/T = 334,000/273.
> Entropy and the arrow of time

explain 4 | Why the past is different from the future
- Microscopic laws (almost) don't care about the direction of time.
- But the universe began in a low-entropy state, and entropy has been rising ever since.
- *Eggs break and don't unbreak because there are vastly more broken arrangements.*
> Next: special relativity properly

## p3-sr | Lorentz transformations | physics | 2 | phys3 | p3-eng
explain 3 | Two postulates rebuild space and time
- 1. Physics is the same in every inertial frame.
- 2. Light's speed in vacuum is the same for every observer.
- From these alone: time dilation, length contraction, and simultaneity that depends on who's watching.
> The Lorentz factor controls everything

quiz 3 | At v = 0.6c, the Lorentz factor γ is…
* 1.25
o 1.6
o 0.8
o 2.5
= γ = 1/√(1 − 0.36) = 1/0.8.
> Simultaneity is relative

story 4 | The train and the platform
- Lightning strikes both ends of a moving train. The platform observer sees both flashes at once.
- The train observer is moving toward one flash: they see that one first, and conclude the strikes weren't simultaneous.
- *Both are right.* "At the same time" depends on the frame.
> Spacetime interval: what everyone agrees on

explain 4 | The spacetime interval is invariant
- $ s² = c²t² − x² − y² − z²
- Observers disagree on times and distances but agree on s².
- *Spacetime geometry with a minus sign.* It's why the travelling twin's path is shorter in proper time.
? What quantity do all inertial observers agree on between two events? :: The spacetime interval c²Δt² − Δx² (− Δy² − Δz²)
> Velocities don't simply add

quiz 4 | A spaceship at 0.5c fires a probe forward at 0.5c relative to the ship. Speed relative to Earth?
* 0.8c
o 1.0c
o 0.5c
o 0.75c
= (u + v)/(1 + uv/c²) = 1/(1 + 0.25) = 0.8c.
> Next: E = mc², carefully

## p3-rmom | Relativistic energy and momentum | physics | 2 | phys3 | p3-sr
explain 3 | The energy-momentum relation
- $ E² = (pc)² + (mc²)²
- At rest: E = mc². Massless particles: E = pc.
- *Photons have momentum but no mass.*
? Relativistic energy-momentum relation? :: E² = (pc)² + (mc²)²
> Nuclear binding energy is missing mass

quiz 3 | Fusing four hydrogen nuclei into helium-4 converts about what fraction of the mass to energy?
* About 0.7%
o About 7%
o About 0.0001%
o About 50%
= Helium-4 is about 0.7% lighter than four protons. The difference powers the Sun.
= The Sun converts about 4 million tonnes of mass into energy every second.
> Matter and antimatter convert 100%

real 4 | PET scans detect annihilation
- A tracer releases positrons. Each annihilates with an electron, making two 511 keV gamma rays in opposite directions.
- 511 keV is exactly the electron's rest energy, mc².
- *The scanner traces back the lines to find where the tracer collected.*
> Next: the birth of quantum theory

## p3-oldq | The quantum revolution | physics | 2 | phys3 | p3-rmom
story 5 | Planck introduced quanta "as an act of desperation"
- Classical physics predicted a hot object should radiate infinite energy at short wavelengths: the ultraviolet catastrophe.
- In 1900 Planck assumed energy comes in chunks E = hf, and the formula matched experiments perfectly.
- *He later described it as an act of desperation.* He didn't believe the chunks were physically real.
> Einstein took the quanta seriously

quiz 3 | Light of 4 eV photons hits a metal with work function 2.5 eV. Max kinetic energy of ejected electrons?
* 1.5 eV
o 6.5 eV
o 4 eV
o 2.5 eV
= Einstein: KE_max = hf − φ.
= Brighter light gives more electrons, not faster ones.
> Compton scattering: photons bounce like billiard balls

explain 4 | Compton scattering
- X-rays scattered off electrons come out with longer wavelengths.
- Δλ = (h/m_ec)(1 − cos θ): exactly what particle collisions with momentum p = h/λ predict.
- *1923: the clinching evidence that photons carry momentum.*
> Bohr's atom quantised the orbits

explain 3 | Bohr's model of hydrogen
- Electrons only allowed in orbits with angular momentum nħ.
- Energies E_n = −13.6 eV/n². Jumps between them emit or absorb specific frequencies.
- *Wrong picture, right energies.* It explained hydrogen's spectral lines exactly.
? Energy levels of hydrogen in the Bohr model? :: Eₙ = −13.6 eV/n²
> de Broglie: matter has a wavelength

quiz 3 | de Broglie wavelength of an electron with momentum p?
* λ = h/p
o λ = p/h
o λ = hp
o λ = h/(mc)
= Confirmed in 1927 by Davisson and Germer, who saw electrons diffract off a nickel crystal.
> Next: the Schrödinger equation
